.\" This document uses ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English.
.TH RESETUSB 8 "July 22, 2026" "resetusb" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
resetusb \- attempt resets for enumerated USB devices on Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B resetusb
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B resetusb
enumerates USB devices through
.BR libusb (3)
and processes each device-list entry.
It attempts a reset only after it reads a device descriptor and opens a non-null handle.
Use it during maintenance or recovery when a USB device does not respond.
It asks libusb to do a USB port reset for every device that it can open.
This reset can reinitialize the device.
If libusb requires re-enumeration, resetusb records a failure and does not
rediscover the device.
.PP
This utility ignores all command-line arguments.
The
.B --help
argument does not show help. It starts the normal reset operation.
It can attempt a reset for each device that it opens. The device list can include hubs.
That can interrupt keyboards, storage devices, serial adapters, and
USB-backed network interfaces.
.PP
Use
.B resetusb
only during maintenance or recovery work where you permit temporary USB
disruption.
.SH OUTPUT
For each successful reset,
.B resetusb
prints a line similar to:
.PP
.EX
reset bus 1 device 2 (1234:5678) Example Device
.EE
.PP
After successful initialization and device enumeration,
.B resetusb
prints a final summary line similar to:
.PP
.EX
Summary: reset 1 device(s), 0 failure(s)
.EE
.PP
The program writes failures to standard error.
A null device entry reports only its list index.
A descriptor failure reports the bus number, device address, and libusb error
name.
An open failure reports the bus number, device address, vendor ID, product ID,
and libusb error name.
A null-handle failure reports the same device fields and the text
.BR "null handle" .
A reset failure also reports the product name.
.PP
Privilege failures do not contain device fields.
Initialization and enumeration failures do not contain device fields.
For a device-specific failure, the program records one failure.
It continues with the next device.
The program does not print a summary after an initialization or enumeration
failure.
.PP
The product name is
.B <unknown>
when the descriptor has no product-string index. It is
.B <string unavailable>
when libusb cannot return the string. The program changes non-printable bytes
to question marks.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All attempted resets completed without failures.
.TP
.B 1
The program returns 1 after a device failure, privilege failure, or invalid
internal call.
It also returns 1 after a libusb initialization or enumeration failure.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.B resetusb
only runs on Linux. Run it as root.
It refuses mismatched real and effective UID execution contexts.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.B resetusb
has no filter, exclusion list, dry-run mode, confirmation step, retry, or
rollback. It processes devices one at a time. It does not quiesce drivers,
unmount file systems, or guarantee device recovery.
.PP
For the complete list, read LIMITATIONS.md in the source or release package.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/resetusb
Installed program path.
.TP
.I /usr/share/man/man8/resetusb.8
Installed manual page path.
.SH SAFETY
Because
.B resetusb
attempts resets for devices that it opens, it can disconnect the peripherals
needed to manage the host.
Plan for console access or another recovery path before you use it on remote
systems.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libusb (3),
.BR lsusb (8)
.SH AUTHORS
resetusb maintainers
.SH HOMEPAGE
Project page:
.UR https://github.com/omkhar/resetusb
https://github.com/omkhar/resetusb
.UE
